  "deviations": [
    {
      "departure": "The manuscript prints the structure of the system matrices but not their entries. The entries in process/regimes.py are the release's own engineering defaults, chosen so the diameter block is Hurwitz, the geometry enters only through the exogenous input u(g_k), and the growth offset reproduces the pooled 2.38 mm per year the manuscript cites.",
      "justification": "The reported numbers cannot be reproduced from a parameterisation the manuscript does not print.",
      "paper_location": "Sec. two-regime model, Eq. (2)-(3)",
      "topic": "generator parameterisation"
    },
    {
      "departure": "With oracle parameters the observation mean is taken to be the latent state, so the boundary-condition and reconstruction machinery fixes the observation covariance and the patient-persistent term supplies the bias. The surrogate therefore enters the generator through the covariance it induces, not through a separate field mean.",
      "justification": "This is what running the filter with the process's own parameters means; it is also the only construction in which the sensor's noise decomposition is the quantity under test.",
      "paper_location": "Eq. (2); Table 1 caption (oracle-parameter filters)",
      "topic": "observation mean under oracle parameters"
    },
    {
      "departure": "The regime-specific process-noise levels, the boundary-condition noise scale, the reconstruction jitter and the class-stratified residual scales are engineering defaults in process/regimes.py, geometry/perturbations.py and sensor/covariance.py.",
      "justification": "The manuscript states the ordering and the decomposition but not the magnitudes.",
      "paper_location": "Sec. two-regime model (small Q_q, larger Q_r); Algorithm 1 line 11",
      "topic": "process noise levels and residual covariance scales"
    },
    {
      "departure": "The stratified annual event risks are a monotone table over the 30-70 mm bands scaled by the four-fold female-to-male ratio the manuscript states, calibrated so the pooled event probability inside the recommended interval lands at the reported order.",
      "justification": "The published strata are cited but not printed; the ratio is the one the manuscript does state.",
      "paper_location": "Eq. (1); Sec. cohort delineation ([8, 44])",
      "topic": "diameter-by-sex event strata"
    },
    {
      "departure": "The literal band schedule is the cited guideline's surveillance schedule (three-yearly below 40 mm, annual at 40-44 mm, three-monthly at 45-54 mm, referral at or above the sex-specific threshold). The manuscript's own literal-band row reports an escalate fraction of 0.000, which this schedule does not produce.",
      "justification": "The schedule the manuscript used is not printed, so its row is reported NOT_RUN rather than reconstructed.",
      "paper_location": "Table 1 panel B; [1] ESVS 2024 guideline",
      "topic": "literal band schedule"
    },
    {
      "departure": "The ablation replaces the decomposed per-scan covariance with the per-channel pooled observation spread on the diagonal and drops the bias coordinates; the manuscript specifies a tuned scalar without defining the tuning.",
      "justification": "A single scalar across channels of different physical units is not identifiable; the per-channel diagonal is the closest honest reading.",
      "paper_location": "Table 2 caption",
      "topic": "tuned scalar noise of the covariance ablation"
    },
    {
      "departure": "History generation and forecasting run on an integer monthly grid, with inter-visit intervals drawn from a logarithmic distribution rounded to whole months and clipped to 3-24 months, so the exact discretisation composes over the interval.",
      "justification": "The intervention grid is reported in whole months; rounding keeps the interval exact and the cohort's median and spread.",
      "paper_location": "Table 4 (inter-observation interval, median 8.6 months)",
      "topic": "observation grid and visit intervals"
    },
    {
      "departure": "The cohort process adds a known site offset to the ultrasound diameter and the filter removes it before the update, because with oracle parameters that offset is part of the process's own parameters.",
      "justification": "Algorithm 2 names delta_US,site in the measurement model; leaving it in would be a specification error rather than a test.",
      "paper_location": "Algorithm 2 line 10",
      "topic": "ultrasound site offset"
    },
    {
      "departure": "The manuscript reports Python 3.9.6 with NumPy 2.0.2. This release targets Python >= 3.10 with NumPy >= 2.0, < 3.",
      "justification": "The reference implementation's interpreter is not available on the verification host.",
      "paper_location": "Code availability",
      "topic": "reported environment"
    },
    {
      "departure": "The shipped configuration defaults are the manuscript's 20 seeds of 300 histories with 256 forecast paths. The verification driver executes a reduced, explicitly labelled scale and reports those numbers as the release's own.",
      "justification": "The full scale is a multi-hour run; every executed number in the report states its scale.",
      "paper_location": "Code availability (python3 bmstf_sim.py --seeds 20 --patients 300 --jobs 5)",
      "topic": "executed in-silico scale"
    },
    {
      "departure": "The fitted visit intensity is a discrete-time per-month hazard with a Gamma prior and light smoothing, stabilised by the unconditional intensity.",
      "justification": "The manuscript specifies the weighting but not the intensity model.",
      "paper_location": "Algorithm 3 line 2",
      "topic": "visit-intensity model"
    },
    {
      "departure": "Sigma_BC and Sigma_rec are sample covariances with the Bessel correction (N_b - 1 and N_m - 1); the manuscript writes cov_s and cov_j without stating the denominator.",
      "justification": "With N_b = 64 and N_m = 8 the unbiased estimator is the standard reading of cov.",
      "paper_location": "Algorithm 1 lines 6 and 10",
      "topic": "sample covariance in the marginalisation"
    }
